  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nSouthwesterly mid-level flow resides over the Central Plains this \nafternoon ahead of a closed low off the California coast. Embedded \nenergy in the mean flow continues to spark spotty showers/sprinkles \nacross the area. A more pronounced wave moves east across Kansas \nthis evening and overnight, with somewhat deeper saturation and\nthe higher chances (20%) for scattered showers. Expect some \nlight rain or sprinkles to continue into Wednesday morning \nbefore dry conditions return. The upper ridge moves overhead \nWednesday through Friday with increasing southerly low-level \nwinds allowing temperatures to warm into the mid and upper 70s \nwith dry conditions. \n\nGuidance is coming into better agreement in the evolution of the \nwestern trough this weekend into early next week. The majority of \nguidance keeps the main trough farther west through the end of the \nweek, leading to a slower progression of the surface cold front. As \nsuch, confidence is increasing in mainly dry and warm conditions \nthrough at least Saturday, potentially even Sunday, before a \nnorthern stream trough shunts the cold front through the area. \nTemperature spreads remain large Sunday into next week given \ndifferences in frontal positioning and cloud cover/precipitation. \nNonetheless, highest precipitation chances come as the front moves \nthrough Sunday-Monday with cooler temperatures expected behind the \nboundary.",
